body:
Same class as #308, in the public `throughput::pipeline` API on next (absent from released main 6e0dffd3).

`pipeline::Stream::new` is safe and public. `submit_batch(batch: Batch<'a>, places: &'a mut [[u8; 32]], tag)` passes raw block/place pointers to the receiver; its soundness comment says the batch and `places` are held by the stream until completion. That holding relies on the stream being finished. Crash-only Drop ends the process, but `mem::forget(stream)` bypasses both.

Compile-only witness, exact ddb7199d7f0b0f04390e923474b630ffc7ff2811 (`safety-pipeline-forget-epyc-20261108T151306-ef36c9f7`, VERIFIED):

```rust
let mut blocks = vec![Block::ZERO; 2];
let mut places = [[0u8; 32]; 2];
let s = Stream::new(Mode::Plain, Limits { buffers: 4, messages: 4 }, |_: Event<'_, u8>| {});
s.submit_batch(Batch::from_blocks(&blocks, 64), &mut places, 0).await;
std::mem::forget(s);
blocks.fill(Block([1; 64]));
drop(blocks);
places[0] = [1; 32];
```

It type-checks. The control placing `s.finish()` after the same release is rejected by the borrow checker, so the forget is what unlocks it. **The async function is never polled; no worker access to freed storage was executed.** A cursory reader sees ordinary borrows and expects Rust to enforce them.

Plausible harm: a producer that leaks or forgets a pipeline (directly, via Rc cycles, or a forgotten owning struct) lets workers read freed input and write into reused caller memory: UB and corrupted unrelated data, from safe code.

Candidate remedies using existing mechanisms, for your design choice: (a) the scoped pattern `throughput::stream` already uses, which finishes before returning and aborts on unwind; or (b) owned batches/places moved in and returned with `Event::Batch`, removing the borrowed lifetime. This is pre-release, so the API change is cheap now.

Full scripts and raw logs will be published with the audit.

— Sam Shackleton
